{"date": "2026-10-22", "post_id": 1497603, "title": "The First Green Star, WD J1330+6435", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-22-the-first-green-star-wd-j13306435/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Star light, star bright: is there a green star out tonight? Other than the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a>, all stars are so far from Earth that they appear as points in the sky.  They are mostly white, but sometimes noticeably <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-december-28-ngc-1898-globular-cluster-in-the-large-magellanic-cloud/\">red or blue</a>. This color typically relates to the temperature: blue stars are hotter than red stars. What about other colors? The <a href=\"https://markgarlick.com/view.php?folder=Space&amp;hash=fb0732935557b8ed4727b65645aa5e23&amp;mediatype=i&amp;type=full\">featured image</a> is an illustration showing <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WD_J1330%2B6435\">WD J1330+6435</a>, a <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs1.html\">white dwarf star</a>, surrounded by an accretion disk with gas and rocks orbiting the star. White dwarfs are <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-16-unexplained-shocks-around-a-white-dwarf-star/\">small, faint stars</a> that are left over when a star like the Sun <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs2.html\">exhausts its nuclear fuel</a>. Astronomers <a href=\"https://warwick.ac.uk/news/pressreleases/astronomers-reveal-true-colours-of-white-dwarf-stars/\">recently found</a> that some white dwarf stars, despite their name, would appear <a href=\"https://warwick.ac.uk/news/pressreleases/astronomers-reveal-true-colours-of-white-dwarf-stars/wd_colours_final_credit_mark-hollands_university-of-warwick.png?maxWidth=1920\">yellow, green, cyan, pink, or purple</a>. In <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/rasti/article/doi/10.1093/rasti/rzag069/8914458\">these cases</a>, trace elements that were accreted to these stars interact with the stellar atmosphere and shift its normally white color. But, alas! I wish I may, I wish I might, but they are too faint to <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1725302592525-376faceb1303\">see tonight</a>... <br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> eye in the sky", "credit": "<a href=\"https://markgarlick.com/\">Mark A. Garlick</a>/<a href=\"https://warwick.ac.uk\">University of Warwick</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://markgarlick.com/\">Mark A. Garlick</a>/<a href=\"https://warwick.ac.uk\">University of Warwick</a>", "alt": "A star shines with a greenish hue in front of a dark field with more distant stars, surrounded by a greenish disk with debris.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-22-the-first-green-star-wd-j13306435/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/green_wd_mark-garlick_annotated2.jpg?w=1280&h=1280&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 22 - The First Green Star, WD J1330+6435</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-22-the-first-green-star-wd-j13306435/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Star light, star bright: is there a green star out tonight? Other than the Sun, all stars are so far from Earth that they appear as points in the sky.  They are mostly white, but sometimes noticeably red or blue. This color typically relates to the temperature: blue stars are hotter than red stars. What about other colors? The featured image is an illustration showing WD J1330+6435, a white dwarf star, surrounded by an accretion disk with gas and rocks orbiting the star. White dwarfs are small, faint stars that are left over when a star like the Sun exhausts its nuclear fuel. Astronomers recently found that some white dwarf stars, despite their name, would appear yellow, green, cyan, pink, or purple. In these cases, trace elements that were accreted to these stars interact with the stellar atmosphere and shift its normally white color. But, alas! I wish I may, I wish I might, but they are too faint to see tonight... Tomorrow&#039;s picture: eye in the sky\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the first green star, wd j1330+6435\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The First Green Star, WD J1330+6435\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-22-the-first-green-star-wd-j13306435/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/green_wd_mark-garlick_annotated2.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=1280&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/green_wd_mark-garlick_annotated2.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=1280&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A star shines with a greenish hue in front of a dark field with more distant stars, surrounded by a greenish disk with debris.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-22T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-21T21:29:11-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The First Green Star, WD J1330+6435\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/green_wd_mark-garlick_annotated2.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=1280&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 22<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/green_wd_mark-garlick_annotated2.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/green_wd_mark-garlick_annotated2.jpg\" alt=\"A star shines with a greenish hue in front of a dark field with more distant stars, surrounded by a greenish disk with debris.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The First Green Star, WD J1330+6435 </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://markgarlick.com/\">Mark A. Garlick</a>/<a href=\"https://warwick.ac.uk\">University of Warwick</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Star light, star bright: is there a green star out tonight? Other than the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a>, all stars are so far from Earth that they appear as points in the sky.  They are mostly white, but sometimes noticeably <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-december-28-ngc-1898-globular-cluster-in-the-large-magellanic-cloud/\">red or blue</a>. This color typically relates to the temperature: blue stars are hotter than red stars. What about other colors? The <a href=\"https://markgarlick.com/view.php?folder=Space&amp;hash=fb0732935557b8ed4727b65645aa5e23&amp;mediatype=i&amp;type=full\">featured image</a> is an illustration showing <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WD_J1330%2B6435\">WD J1330+6435</a>, a <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs1.html\">white dwarf star</a>, surrounded by an accretion disk with gas and rocks orbiting the star. White dwarfs are <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-16-unexplained-shocks-around-a-white-dwarf-star/\">small, faint stars</a> that are left over when a star like the Sun <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs2.html\">exhausts its nuclear fuel</a>. Astronomers <a href=\"https://warwick.ac.uk/news/pressreleases/astronomers-reveal-true-colours-of-white-dwarf-stars/\">recently found</a> that some white dwarf stars, despite their name, would appear <a href=\"https://warwick.ac.uk/news/pressreleases/astronomers-reveal-true-colours-of-white-dwarf-stars/wd_colours_final_credit_mark-hollands_university-of-warwick.png?maxWidth=1920\">yellow, green, cyan, pink, or purple</a>. In <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/rasti/article/doi/10.1093/rasti/rzag069/8914458\">these cases</a>, trace elements that were accreted to these stars interact with the stellar atmosphere and shift its normally white color. But, alas! I wish I may, I wish I might, but they are too faint to <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1725302592525-376faceb1303\">see tonight</a>... <br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> eye in the sky\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261022/html"}